1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,360
THEME SONG: In the not too
 distant future, somewhere

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:10,360
in time and space, Mike
 Nelson and his robot pals

3
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:13,320
are caught in a nasty place.

4
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:18,020
They try to survive the wrath
 of Pearl, just an evil gal who

5
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:19,250
wants to rule the world.

6
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:22,710
From her castle below,
 she sets her sights above,

7
00:00:22,710 --> 00:00:26,170
just to torture all the captives
 on the Satellite of Love.

8
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:29,130
Get me down!

9
00:00:29,130 --> 00:00:34,070
I'll send him cheesy movies,
 the worst I can find.

10
00:00:34,070 --> 00:00:35,060
La, la, la.

11
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:38,020
He'll have to sit
 and watch them all.

12
00:00:38,020 --> 00:00:40,460
And I'll monitor his mind.

13
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:41,460
La, la, la.

14
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:44,450
Now, keep in mind
 Mike can't control

15
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:46,950
where the movies begin or end.

16
00:00:46,950 --> 00:00:47,450
La, la, la.

17
00:00:47,450 --> 00:00:52,440
He'll try to keep his sanity
 with the help of his robot

18
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:55,430
friends.

19
00:00:55,430 --> 00:00:57,260
Robot roll call.

20
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:01,130
Cambot-- you're on-- Gypsy-- oh
 my gosh-- Tom Servo-- check me

21
00:01:01,130 --> 00:01:03,230
out-- Crow-- I'm different.

22
00:01:03,230 --> 00:01:06,500
If you're wondering how
 he eats and breathes

23
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:09,300
and other science
 facts-- la, la, la--

24
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:12,280
just repeat to yourself,
 it's just a show.

25
00:01:12,280 --> 00:01:33,650
I should really just relax for
 "Mystery Science Theater 3000."

26
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:37,090
-(SINGING) Ooh, don't you
 know, doo, doo, doo, doo.

27
00:01:37,090 --> 00:01:38,600
Oh, hey, Mike.

28
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:40,040
I see you've got
 today's "Boston Globe."

29
00:01:40,040 --> 00:01:41,010
-Yeah, Crow.

30
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:43,370
I didn't know you had
 a syndicated column

31
00:01:43,370 --> 00:01:46,340
in the lifestyle section
 of many major newspapers.

32
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:47,730
-Oh, sure.

33
00:01:47,730 --> 00:01:50,890
I was inspired by Larry
 King's column in "USA Today."

34
00:01:50,890 --> 00:01:53,290
-"My $0.2, by Crow T Robot."

35
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:53,910
-Yeah.

36
00:01:53,910 --> 00:01:55,700
-"I said it before,
 and I'll say it again.

37
00:01:55,700 --> 00:01:58,200
Shoelaces are handy,
 even necessary,

38
00:01:58,200 --> 00:01:59,690
to many of today's shoe styles."

39
00:01:59,690 --> 00:02:00,810
-Yeah.

40
00:02:00,810 --> 00:02:03,540
-"Volleyball player Gabrielle
 Reece is the best athlete

41
00:02:03,540 --> 00:02:06,620
in the world in arguably the
 most difficult of all sports."

42
00:02:06,620 --> 00:02:07,270
-Right.

43
00:02:07,270 --> 00:02:10,280
-"The Boeing 727 is still
 my favorite aircraft."

44
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:11,530
-Mm-hmm.

45
00:02:11,530 --> 00:02:14,850
-"I give Supreme Court Justice
 Antonin Scalia a B plus."

46
00:02:14,850 --> 00:02:15,710
-Yep.

47
00:02:15,710 --> 00:02:17,340
-"Television shows
 aired last night."

48
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:19,290
Crow, I have to say,
 this column doesn't

49
00:02:19,290 --> 00:02:21,540
seem particularly substantive.

50
00:02:21,540 --> 00:02:23,290
-Oh, thanks, Mike.

51
00:02:23,290 --> 00:02:24,770
You should have seen
 last Tuesday's column.

52
00:02:24,770 --> 00:02:27,100
I remarked on a patty
 melt I had for lunch.

53
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:28,030
-Must have been fascinating.

54
00:02:28,030 --> 00:02:30,030
Now, who reads this column?

55
00:02:30,030 --> 00:02:31,090
[HUMS]

56
00:02:31,090 --> 00:02:32,690
-You, for one.

57
00:02:32,690 --> 00:02:33,300
-I just read it.

58
00:02:33,300 --> 00:02:35,320
-Crow, you were right
 on about shoelaces, man.

59
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:37,340
I don't know what we'd do
 without journalists like you

60
00:02:37,340 --> 00:02:39,110
tellin' it like it is.

61
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:40,120
-We'll be right back.

62
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:40,750
-Wow.

63
00:02:40,750 --> 00:02:42,690
Television shows
 did air last night!

64
00:02:42,690 --> 00:02:43,190
[CHUCKLES]

65
00:02:43,190 --> 00:02:43,990
-Man!

66
00:02:43,990 --> 00:02:50,880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

67
00:02:50,880 --> 00:02:52,950
-OK, Mike, is this
 more substantial?

68
00:02:52,950 --> 00:02:56,790
"Could there be more goose
 poop in my front yard?"

69
00:02:56,790 --> 00:02:57,940
-No.

70
00:02:57,940 --> 00:02:59,430
-No, it's not more substantial?

71
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:01,530
Or no, there could not be
 more goose poop in my--

72
00:03:01,530 --> 00:03:03,660
-Let's see what
 Pearl's up to, OK?

73
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:06,190
-I just wanted an answer.

74
00:03:06,190 --> 00:03:07,630
[THUNDER]

75
00:03:12,270 --> 00:03:12,770
-Huh?

76
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:13,400
-What the--

77
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:14,130
-What's going on?
 -Hey!

78
00:03:14,130 --> 00:03:14,800
Hey, look at this.

79
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:16,060
Another button.

80
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:17,600
-I never noticed
 that before-- Hello?

81
00:03:21,780 --> 00:03:22,750
[GRUNT]

82
00:03:27,640 --> 00:03:29,000
-Oh, hi, Mike.

83
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,070
I'm sorry.

84
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:32,720
I should have told you we
 were moving the castle.

85
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:34,690
I didn't mean to
 leave you home alone.

86
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:39,130
Well, anyway, we had to.

87
00:03:39,130 --> 00:03:44,050
I finally got sick of paying $50
 a year for my monkey license.

88
00:03:44,050 --> 00:03:46,080
-Uh, where do you
 want the silverware?

89
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:49,470
-Well, in the kitchen
 where it always goes.

90
00:03:49,470 --> 00:03:51,160
$50.

91
00:03:51,160 --> 00:03:53,570
I'm not gonna pay
 the government $50

92
00:03:53,570 --> 00:03:56,300
for the privilege
 of owning a monkey.

93
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:59,170
How much do you pay for
 your little things up there?

94
00:03:59,170 --> 00:04:01,490
-Well, not that much.

95
00:04:01,490 --> 00:04:02,470
-Really?

96
00:04:02,470 --> 00:04:03,610
-Exactly.

97
00:04:03,610 --> 00:04:06,170
So anyway, we're living
 in a much better place.

98
00:04:06,170 --> 00:04:07,150
It's quieter.

99
00:04:07,150 --> 00:04:09,580
Frankly, it's a
 better neighborhood.

100
00:04:09,580 --> 00:04:14,170
And they only charge $10 a
 year for the monkey license.

101
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:15,470
Pretty shrewd, huh?

102
00:04:15,470 --> 00:04:16,720
-And it's a nice one, too.

103
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:19,380
Look, it's red.

104
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:20,070
-Wow.

105
00:04:20,070 --> 00:04:21,730
Real neat.

106
00:04:21,730 --> 00:04:25,330
Does seem like kind of a
 commotion just to save the $40,

107
00:04:25,330 --> 00:04:26,470
but you know, what do I know?

108
00:04:26,470 --> 00:04:26,970
-Yeah.

109
00:04:26,970 --> 00:04:27,610
-Right?

110
00:04:27,610 --> 00:04:30,840
-Oh no, Mike, it only cost $2.3
 million to move the castle.

111
00:04:30,840 --> 00:04:34,720
So it'll pay off in
 about 3,512 years,

112
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:36,310
assuming 2% inflation each year.

113
00:04:36,310 --> 00:04:38,950
So you see how much
 sense it makes.

114
00:04:38,950 --> 00:04:40,400
-Well, maybe if
 you'd moved it with

115
00:04:40,400 --> 00:04:41,770
that big deal brain of yours--

116
00:04:41,770 --> 00:04:44,020
-I'm not that omnipotent, Pearl.

117
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:45,790
And besides, we, we
 packed everything up,

118
00:04:45,790 --> 00:04:48,450
moved the castle here, only
 to unpack it and put it

119
00:04:48,450 --> 00:04:49,800
back again in the same place.

120
00:04:49,800 --> 00:04:51,190
I mean, why?

121
00:04:51,190 --> 00:04:53,290
-Oh, I actually have a
 pretty good reason for that.

122
00:04:53,290 --> 00:04:53,790
-OK.

123
00:04:53,790 --> 00:04:55,050
-It's because--

124
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:56,610
-Ow, oh.

125
00:04:56,610 --> 00:04:57,370
Oh.

126
00:04:57,370 --> 00:04:58,670
-It's real official, too.

127
00:04:58,670 --> 00:04:59,250
Look.

128
00:04:59,250 --> 00:05:03,250
"For control of parasite- and
 disease-riddled smelly apes."

129
00:05:03,250 --> 00:05:04,420
That's me.

130
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:05,050
-Go lay down, Bobo.

131
00:05:05,050 --> 00:05:06,040
Lay down.

132
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:07,200
As for you up there--

133
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,310
-Oh, I'd imagine that,
 what with all the moving,

134
00:05:09,310 --> 00:05:11,460
you don't even have time for
 a movie for us today, do you?

135
00:05:11,460 --> 00:05:12,140
-Nah.
 -Huh?

136
00:05:12,140 --> 00:05:12,640
-Nah.

137
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:13,330
-Huh?

138
00:05:13,330 --> 00:05:14,410
-Yeah, right.

139
00:05:14,410 --> 00:05:15,490
Oh, no movie.

140
00:05:15,490 --> 00:05:18,210
Oh, except for this one.

141
00:05:18,210 --> 00:05:19,880
And it's a really bad movie.

142
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,690
It's called "Horrors
 of Spider Island."

143
00:05:21,690 --> 00:05:22,550
I hope that's OK.

144
00:05:22,550 --> 00:05:23,350
[DOGS BARKING OFFSCREEN]

145
00:05:23,350 --> 00:05:24,860
[BOBO BARKING]

146
00:05:24,860 --> 00:05:26,860
-Real peaceful in the
 new neighborhood, too.

147
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:27,640
-Hey, shh.

148
00:05:27,640 --> 00:05:28,630
No!

149
00:05:28,630 --> 00:05:30,110
[BARKING]

150
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:32,090
-Gah.

151
00:05:32,090 --> 00:05:33,560
[ROBOTS BARKING]

152
00:05:33,560 --> 00:05:34,060
-Ah, no.

153
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:35,540
Not the chain bark.

154
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:36,530
And we got movie sign.

155
00:05:36,530 --> 00:05:37,520
Come on.

156
00:05:48,390 --> 00:05:50,610
-"Horrors of Spider Island."

157
00:05:50,610 --> 00:05:52,730
-Hey, gi-give us a
 chance to sit down.

158
00:05:52,730 --> 00:05:53,230
-Yeah.

159
00:05:53,230 --> 00:05:55,530
What's going on?

160
00:05:55,530 --> 00:05:57,370
-[SUGGESTIVE_CHUCKLE]

161
00:05:57,370 --> 00:06:01,270
-It's an anagram for
 "a bra bra navel nite."

162
00:06:01,270 --> 00:06:02,620
-[LAUGHS]

163
00:06:02,620 --> 00:06:07,080
-Ah, Temple Foster, where
 they worship Australian beer.

164
00:06:07,080 --> 00:06:09,260
-Hey, they're playing
 make-out music, Mike.

165
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:11,040
What do you say?

166
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:12,020
-Get out of here.
 -Come on.

167
00:06:12,020 --> 00:06:12,520
-Hm?

168
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:13,020
Hm?

169
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:14,490
Hm?

170
00:06:14,490 --> 00:06:16,450
-No.

171
00:06:16,450 --> 00:06:18,560
Now, too bad we can grab
 this movie with a tissue

172
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:23,160
and crumple it and flush
 it down the toilet.

173
00:06:23,160 --> 00:06:24,570
Suppose we could.

174
00:06:24,570 --> 00:06:25,090
-Pacemaker.

175
00:06:25,090 --> 00:06:27,240
Ironically, they used a lot
 of microwave ovens and metal

176
00:06:27,240 --> 00:06:28,950
detectors during this movie.

177
00:06:28,950 --> 00:06:29,450
Heh.

178
00:06:33,590 --> 00:06:35,700
-Well.

179
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:38,230
-Spider Island,
 home to the stars.

180
00:06:38,230 --> 00:06:39,710
-Woo.

181
00:06:39,710 --> 00:06:42,060
-This is back when
 every car was filled

182
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:43,520
with a private investigator.

183
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:46,760
-[SINGS_WITH_BACKGROUND_MUSIC]

184
00:06:46,760 --> 00:06:51,220
-Boy, Spider Island's
 all built up now.

185
00:06:51,220 --> 00:06:53,640
-Hey, that guy took
 his boat on land.

186
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:54,600
-Wow.

187
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:58,480
[SINGS WITH BACKGROUND MUSIC]

188
00:06:58,480 --> 00:06:59,610
-(SINGING) Do the parallel park.

189
00:06:59,610 --> 00:07:00,900
It's the parallel park.

190
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:02,830
Do the park, park,
 parky, park, park.

191
00:07:02,830 --> 00:07:03,820
-(WHISPERS) Yeah.

192
00:07:03,820 --> 00:07:05,660
-It's a dames and
 broads audition.

193
00:07:08,410 --> 00:07:09,850
[BURPS]

194
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:18,960
-Hey, nice office
 for an enlisted man.

195
00:07:18,960 --> 00:07:22,650
-Here's Singapore, in
 the south of Malaya,

196
00:07:22,650 --> 00:07:25,730
population
 approximately 550,000.

197
00:07:25,730 --> 00:07:26,230
-Yeek.

198
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:30,040
-University shipbuilding,
 important sea port.

199
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:33,500
-Doesn't it say anything
 about the 32 nightclubs there?

200
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:35,410
-Have you already worked
 down in Singapore?

201
00:07:35,410 --> 00:07:36,310
-No.

202
00:07:36,310 --> 00:07:37,570
-Not to work.

203
00:07:37,570 --> 00:07:39,740
However, I once had a
 friend, an oil sheik.

204
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:41,630
He took me with him down there.

205
00:07:41,630 --> 00:07:43,070
He was quite a guy.

206
00:07:43,070 --> 00:07:44,040
[CHUCKLES]

207
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:46,760
-Those are the guys that run
 around in turbans, aren't they?

208
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:47,970
-Listen, honey.

209
00:07:47,970 --> 00:07:49,270
When they take
 those turbans off,

210
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:50,720
they're just like any other man.

211
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:51,510
-Turbanless.

212
00:07:51,510 --> 00:07:53,810
-Got a light?

213
00:07:53,810 --> 00:07:54,310
Ah.

214
00:07:56,940 --> 00:07:58,880
-Lucky sign.

215
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:01,320
-Looking forward to
 going to Singapore?

216
00:08:01,320 --> 00:08:03,660
-Hm, I don't really
 care where I dance,

217
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:04,930
as long as the pay's good.

218
00:08:04,930 --> 00:08:06,330
[BUZZER]

219
00:08:06,330 --> 00:08:09,130
-'Scuse me.

220
00:08:09,130 --> 00:08:11,230
-I'm Bob Box-body.

221
00:08:11,230 --> 00:08:15,160
-That's him now.

222
00:08:15,160 --> 00:08:17,060
-I mind very much if you smoke.

223
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:24,560
-Hey, quit doing your
 Sharon Stone impersonation.

224
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:25,060
-Hi.

225
00:08:25,060 --> 00:08:27,780
-Hi.

226
00:08:27,780 --> 00:08:29,650
-I've already been to Singapore.

227
00:08:29,650 --> 00:08:30,810
-And you look like it.

228
00:08:30,810 --> 00:08:32,790
-Oh, Gary.

229
00:08:32,790 --> 00:08:38,100
-(CHUCKLING) (IMITATING GARY)
 I said, "And you look like it."

230
00:08:38,100 --> 00:08:38,600
-Ah.

231
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:40,030
-Dr. Strangelove!

232
00:08:40,030 --> 00:08:41,250
-Hello, Gary.

233
00:08:41,250 --> 00:08:41,900
Hi, Georgia.

234
00:08:41,900 --> 00:08:43,820
You're looking
 prettier every day.

235
00:08:43,820 --> 00:08:45,080
How in the world are you doing?

236
00:08:45,080 --> 00:08:45,970
-Mike?

237
00:08:45,970 --> 00:08:46,660
-Yeah?

238
00:08:46,660 --> 00:08:48,920
-Have you told the girls
 what this is all about?

239
00:08:48,920 --> 00:08:49,850
-Sure, Gary.

240
00:08:49,850 --> 00:08:53,110
A tour for a dancing troupe,
 first engagement, Singapore.

241
00:08:53,110 --> 00:08:54,920
I've 12 of them
 here to pick from.

242
00:08:54,920 --> 00:08:55,770
-Enjoy, Gary.

243
00:08:55,770 --> 00:08:56,610
-OK, Georgia.

244
00:08:56,610 --> 00:08:57,440
Let the girls in.

245
00:08:57,440 --> 00:08:59,100
We don't have much time.

246
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:00,600
-All right if we've
 start now, Mr. Blackwood?

247
00:09:00,600 --> 00:09:01,270
-Of course.

248
00:09:01,270 --> 00:09:02,810
[GRUNTS]

249
00:09:02,810 --> 00:09:04,660
-Oh, but one at a
 time for, please.

250
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:06,090
-OK.

251
00:09:06,090 --> 00:09:06,870
May?

252
00:09:06,870 --> 00:09:09,910
-We're looking for someone
 who will sleep with us.

253
00:09:09,910 --> 00:09:11,040
-Smile, will ya?

254
00:09:11,040 --> 00:09:12,180
-How do you do?

255
00:09:12,180 --> 00:09:13,640
That's Mr. Webster, the manager.

256
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:15,120
And this is May.

257
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:16,730
She's the finest dancer in town.

258
00:09:16,730 --> 00:09:18,720
-See?

259
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:20,160
-What was your last engagement?

260
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:21,830
-With the Coquettes, one year.

261
00:09:21,830 --> 00:09:23,060
-Ah, one of the finest.

262
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:23,620
-Mm.

263
00:09:23,620 --> 00:09:25,230
Legs?

264
00:09:25,230 --> 00:09:26,360
-Pardon me?

265
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:28,040
-Mr. Webster would like
 to look at your legs.

266
00:09:28,040 --> 00:09:30,770
-Oh, sure.

267
00:09:30,770 --> 00:09:31,840
-Suppress it, suppress it.

268
00:09:31,840 --> 00:09:32,340
-Thank you.

269
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:33,050
You're hired.

270
00:09:33,050 --> 00:09:34,160
Please wait outside.

271
00:09:34,160 --> 00:09:36,880
-Oh, thank you.

272
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:37,380
-Hm.

273
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:39,410
May, Coquettes.

274
00:09:39,410 --> 00:09:41,680
-And this is Rhonda Hunter.

275
00:09:41,680 --> 00:09:42,570
-Dancer?

276
00:09:42,570 --> 00:09:44,410
-Not really.

277
00:09:44,410 --> 00:09:46,620
But I pick things up real fast.

278
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:48,480
-Hm.

279
00:09:48,480 --> 00:09:50,690
-(FALSETTO) Well, let's
 see your clavicle.

280
00:09:50,690 --> 00:09:51,190
-Thank you.

281
00:09:51,190 --> 00:09:52,500
We're looking for dancers.

282
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:53,920
-But what's that got
 to do with Singapore?

283
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:54,420
-Come on.

284
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:54,920
Come on, honey.

285
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:55,740
Don't hold us up.

286
00:09:55,740 --> 00:09:58,320
-This is how "Ms.
 Magazine" does its hiring.

287
00:09:58,320 --> 00:10:00,040
-Babs?

288
00:10:00,040 --> 00:10:02,450
-Babs played fullback
 for the Lions.

289
00:10:02,450 --> 00:10:03,760
-This is Babs.

290
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:06,010
Her legs are worth
 their weight in gold.

291
00:10:06,010 --> 00:10:08,210
-Can those golden
 legs also dance?

292
00:10:08,210 --> 00:10:11,230
-I was solo dancer in
 the Apollo Variety.

293
00:10:11,230 --> 00:10:13,470
-And why did you leave there?

294
00:10:13,470 --> 00:10:15,210
-Well, the boss
 really went for her,

295
00:10:15,210 --> 00:10:18,580
but his wife thought otherwise.

296
00:10:18,580 --> 00:10:20,380
-There it is again!

297
00:10:20,380 --> 00:10:22,160
-All right, you can
 work with as long

298
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:23,940
as you don't have any affairs.

299
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:26,270
Mr. Webster doesn't
 tolerate any fooling around.

300
00:10:26,270 --> 00:10:27,580
-Yeah, that's cool.

301
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:29,910
-I had all the boys I can take.

302
00:10:29,910 --> 00:10:31,820
-Come on.

303
00:10:31,820 --> 00:10:33,030
Carolyn?

304
00:10:33,030 --> 00:10:35,180
-(OLD WOMAN VOICE) Is
 this the Ensure auditions?

305
00:10:35,180 --> 00:10:37,440
-This little one's from
 the National Ballet.

306
00:10:37,440 --> 00:10:38,810
-Could you dance a
 little bit for us?

307
00:10:38,810 --> 00:10:39,310
-Glad to.

308
00:10:39,310 --> 00:10:40,420
Would you put this
 one on for me?

309
00:10:40,420 --> 00:10:40,920
-Sure.

310
00:10:40,920 --> 00:10:42,300
Let's have a look.

311
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:43,570
-But none of that modern stuff.

312
00:10:43,570 --> 00:10:44,570
Just shake your dinners.

313
00:10:49,050 --> 00:10:51,280
-(FALSETTO) This is called
 "You'll Never Have Sex With

314
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:54,860
Me."

315
00:10:54,860 --> 00:10:56,000
-Thank goodness
 she has that belt,

316
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,010
or her outfit would
 fall right off.

317
00:11:01,970 --> 00:11:03,450
-Well, boss, we
 can always pay her

318
00:11:03,450 --> 00:11:05,130
in cigarettes,
 cocaine, and coffee.

319
00:11:07,750 --> 00:11:10,660
-"The Horrors of Spider Island."

320
00:11:10,660 --> 00:11:12,470
-He's supported by
 a generous grant

321
00:11:12,470 --> 00:11:14,630
from the Onan Foundation
 and the Chubb Group.

322
00:11:19,680 --> 00:11:21,130
-Oh, she made him vomit.

323
00:11:21,130 --> 00:11:21,630
Ugh.

324
00:11:25,060 --> 00:11:26,740
-All right, can
 the crap, Giselle.

325
00:11:29,250 --> 00:11:33,340
[WHIRRING]

326
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:35,020
-Thank you, Carolyn.

327
00:11:35,020 --> 00:11:36,250
You dance wonderfully.

328
00:11:36,250 --> 00:11:36,980
-For a cow.

329
00:11:36,980 --> 00:11:39,260
-But I'm afraid ballet is
 not what we're looking for.

330
00:11:39,260 --> 00:11:41,420
-(FALSETTO) I guess it's back
 to my dead-end job as principal

331
00:11:41,420 --> 00:11:42,960
dancer with the Bolshoi.

332
00:11:42,960 --> 00:11:44,340
-Don't be said, honey.

333
00:11:44,340 --> 00:11:47,030
I tell you what, uh,
 come in again tomorrow.

334
00:11:47,030 --> 00:11:47,530
-Yeah?

335
00:11:47,530 --> 00:11:48,030
-Yeah.

336
00:11:48,030 --> 00:11:49,150
I've got something else for you.

337
00:11:49,150 --> 00:11:50,900
-Lunch and gossip.

338
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:53,030
-All right.

339
00:11:53,030 --> 00:11:55,930
-It's a dancer and
 her seeing eye dancer.

340
00:11:55,930 --> 00:11:57,260
-Here are Gladys and Doreen.

341
00:11:57,260 --> 00:11:57,870
-(FALSETTO) Hello.

342
00:11:57,870 --> 00:12:00,500
-I told you to let
 them in separately.

343
00:12:00,500 --> 00:12:03,330
-These babes've been working
 from the start as a duo number.

344
00:12:03,330 --> 00:12:05,980
-And we would very much like
 to stay together if possible.

345
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:07,740
-On this tour, you'll be
 dancing with other girls.

346
00:12:07,740 --> 00:12:11,500
-That doesn't matter, as long
 as we get to go overseas.

347
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:12,300
-All right.

348
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:13,320
You can both work with us.

349
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:14,450
Please send the next one in.

350
00:12:14,450 --> 00:12:14,950
-Yeah.

351
00:12:14,950 --> 00:12:15,790
-Thank you.

352
00:12:15,790 --> 00:12:16,370
-See you.

353
00:12:16,370 --> 00:12:19,280
-The Indigo Girls of dancing.

354
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:19,770
-Linda?

355
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:24,290
-Hello, there.

356
00:12:24,290 --> 00:12:25,390
-It's Barney Allen.

357
00:12:28,320 --> 00:12:29,230
-Hm.

358
00:12:29,230 --> 00:12:30,290
You're a stripper?

359
00:12:30,290 --> 00:12:32,020
-And refinisher, yes.

360
00:12:32,020 --> 00:12:32,520
-Yeah.

361
00:12:32,520 --> 00:12:33,770
-Sometimes.

362
00:12:33,770 --> 00:12:36,110
-Didn't I see you work the
 Sapphire Club last month?

363
00:12:36,110 --> 00:12:38,650
[GASPING]

364
00:12:38,650 --> 00:12:41,660
-How come you quit that place?

365
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:43,260
-The boss wanted to.

366
00:12:43,260 --> 00:12:44,930
I didn't.

367
00:12:44,930 --> 00:12:45,690
-Oh.

368
00:12:45,690 --> 00:12:47,330
Are you always so picayunish?

369
00:12:47,330 --> 00:12:48,960
-No, I'm Lithuanian.

370
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:52,580
-That depends on the boss, boss.

371
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:55,320
-Shall I dance for you?

372
00:12:55,320 --> 00:12:56,870
-Yes, for god's sake, yes!

373
00:12:56,870 --> 00:12:58,700
-It's not necessary.

374
00:12:58,700 --> 00:12:59,710
You're hired.

375
00:12:59,710 --> 00:13:00,700
-(WHISPERS) Thank you.

376
00:13:00,700 --> 00:13:01,910
-Wait outside.

377
00:13:01,910 --> 00:13:02,910
-OK.

378
00:13:02,910 --> 00:13:03,950
-Well, it's a nice day.

379
00:13:03,950 --> 00:13:06,490
I'm going to go home like this.

380
00:13:06,490 --> 00:13:07,830
-Next girl, please.

381
00:13:07,830 --> 00:13:09,300
-Strip, come on!

382
00:13:09,300 --> 00:13:10,080
-Hey, hey, easy.

383
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:11,330
-My name is Nelly Hastings.

384
00:13:11,330 --> 00:13:12,950
-A new one, I haven't
 seen her work yet,

385
00:13:12,950 --> 00:13:14,550
but she's always been
 with good troupes.

386
00:13:14,550 --> 00:13:17,000
Let's see her dance.

387
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:17,710
-Great, that's good.

388
00:13:17,710 --> 00:13:18,210
You're hired.

389
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:26,330
-Pat Nixon gets down
 at the White House.

390
00:13:26,330 --> 00:13:27,810
-Cha!

391
00:13:27,810 --> 00:13:31,250
-She studied under Rose Marie.

392
00:13:31,250 --> 00:13:33,540
[SINGS ALONG TO MUSIC]

393
00:13:33,540 --> 00:13:37,900
-I think her timing
 belt is too tight.

394
00:13:37,900 --> 00:13:39,080
-Well, look out, Singapore.

395
00:13:42,260 --> 00:13:44,820
-Brought to you by the
 National Garter Council.

396
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:45,320
-Wear garters.

397
00:13:48,380 --> 00:13:50,740
-It's the drunk aunt
 at the wedding dance.

398
00:13:54,230 --> 00:13:57,250
-And so, it just
 goes on like that.

399
00:13:57,250 --> 00:13:58,580
[SINGS ALONG TO MUSIC]

400
00:13:58,580 --> 00:14:00,050
-Thank you.

401
00:14:00,050 --> 00:14:01,530
You're hired.

402
00:14:01,530 --> 00:14:03,280
Please wait outside.

403
00:14:03,280 --> 00:14:03,780
-Nice.

404
00:14:03,780 --> 00:14:04,410
-Doo, doo-doo, doo.

405
00:14:04,410 --> 00:14:11,350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

406
00:14:11,350 --> 00:14:13,810
-So Mr. Blackwood has supplied
 all the girls we need.

407
00:14:13,810 --> 00:14:14,940
-Fine, Georgia.

408
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:16,760
Go and tell the girls
 when costume and dance

409
00:14:16,760 --> 00:14:18,920
rehearsals will start,
 will you please?

410
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:19,470
-All right.

411
00:14:19,470 --> 00:14:21,730
-Like my transition lenses?

412
00:14:21,730 --> 00:14:22,610
-Listen, Gary.

413
00:14:22,610 --> 00:14:23,780
I thought you were the boss.

414
00:14:23,780 --> 00:14:26,250
But you let Georgia
 select all the girls.

415
00:14:26,250 --> 00:14:28,200
-I select all the girls.

416
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:29,210
-You?

417
00:14:29,210 --> 00:14:30,570
But you didn't say a word.

418
00:14:30,570 --> 00:14:31,610
-Why should I?

419
00:14:31,610 --> 00:14:33,390
I have a little trick.

420
00:14:33,390 --> 00:14:35,120
When I keep my legs
 apart like so--

421
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:35,620
-Ah.

422
00:14:35,620 --> 00:14:36,120
[GRUNTS]

423
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:37,200
---it means I'm not interested.

424
00:14:37,200 --> 00:14:40,310
When I cross them like
 this, ah, then it's OK.

425
00:14:40,310 --> 00:14:42,290
-Ah.

426
00:14:42,290 --> 00:14:44,280
-You always were a crazy guy.

427
00:14:44,280 --> 00:14:45,500
When do things get underway?

428
00:14:45,500 --> 00:14:47,790
-I'm having my body
 re-blocked tomorrow.

429
00:14:47,790 --> 00:14:48,730
-10 days.

430
00:14:48,730 --> 00:14:49,540
-10 days?

431
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:52,010
Well then, have a good trip.

432
00:14:52,010 --> 00:14:54,630
-Thank you.

433
00:14:54,630 --> 00:14:57,670
-Hundreds of dancers are
 loaded to a C31 transport

434
00:14:57,670 --> 00:14:59,410
and dropped on Singapore.

435
00:14:59,410 --> 00:15:01,220
-Doo, doo-doo, doo-doo, doo dah.

436
00:15:01,220 --> 00:15:03,980
-You know, I never get to
 fly with exotic dancers, just

437
00:15:03,980 --> 00:15:05,960
seed salesmen from La Crosse.

438
00:15:10,330 --> 00:15:14,030
-Hm, Singapore's, right
 across the Hudson River.

439
00:15:14,030 --> 00:15:16,160
-From Los Angeles, they
 take off from New York

440
00:15:16,160 --> 00:15:16,790
to go to Singapore.

441
00:15:23,350 --> 00:15:23,850
-Mm-hmm.

442
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:27,390
OK, there.

443
00:15:27,390 --> 00:15:29,460
I've looked at clouds
 from both sides now.

444
00:15:29,460 --> 00:15:29,960
And you know?

445
00:15:29,960 --> 00:15:31,180
Frankly, I'm not that impressed.

446
00:15:35,080 --> 00:15:37,860
-Mm, this is a good storm, but
 it's not the perfect storm.

447
00:15:41,770 --> 00:15:43,960
-Oh, they had to divert
 around Charlie Rich's hair.

448
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:47,530
CAPTAIN (ON PA
 SYSTEM): We are now

449
00:15:47,530 --> 00:15:50,270
flying over the Pacific
 Ocean, near Honolulu.

450
00:15:50,270 --> 00:15:52,310
Altitude is 15,000 feet.

451
00:15:52,310 --> 00:15:53,520
-Wait, 1,500.

452
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:54,130
Aw, crap.

453
00:15:54,130 --> 00:15:56,760
Does anyone know how
 to read these things?

454
00:15:56,760 --> 00:15:58,330
CAPTAIN (ON PA SYSTEM):
 Mayday, mayday, mayday.

455
00:15:58,330 --> 00:16:00,060
Number 247, number 247.

456
00:16:00,060 --> 00:16:00,720
-(WITH EFFORT) May--

457
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:02,630
CAPTAIN (ON PA SYSTEM): We're
 9,000 feet and losing altitude.

458
00:16:02,630 --> 00:16:03,240
---day.

459
00:16:03,240 --> 00:16:04,070
CAPTAIN (ON PA SYSTEM):
 Number two engine burning.

460
00:16:04,070 --> 00:16:04,950
-Wait, slow down!

461
00:16:04,950 --> 00:16:06,720
CAPTAIN (ON PA SYSTEM):
 Attempting emergency landing.

462
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:08,680
Present position is--

463
00:16:08,680 --> 00:16:09,980
-I think they'll pull out of it.

464
00:16:09,980 --> 00:16:11,250
-Repeat your position!

465
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:12,280
Repeat your position!

466
00:16:12,280 --> 00:16:13,020
-I'm sitting.

467
00:16:13,020 --> 00:16:16,220
-Damn, I can't pick
 him up anymore.

468
00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:18,080
Clipper 247--

469
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:20,680
-So is the beverage
 service over?

470
00:16:20,680 --> 00:16:25,060
[CHUCKLES]

471
00:16:25,060 --> 00:16:27,470
-I Cliniqued!

472
00:16:27,470 --> 00:16:27,970
-Ow.

473
00:16:31,380 --> 00:16:34,100
-Oh, Abby's running more
 "how we met" stories.

474
00:16:37,310 --> 00:16:40,090
-No, Mr. Hastings,
 I can only repeat.

475
00:16:40,090 --> 00:16:43,340
There's absolutely no reason
 yet to fear the worst.

476
00:16:43,340 --> 00:16:46,310
Until now, we only know
 that the plane caught fire

477
00:16:46,310 --> 00:16:48,030
and that we've
 lost radio contact.

478
00:16:48,030 --> 00:16:50,900
-Well, there's absolutely
 nothing to be worried about.

479
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:51,400
-Yeah.

480
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:52,790
Yeah, I'll let you
 know immediately,

481
00:16:52,790 --> 00:16:55,220
soon as I hear something new.

482
00:16:55,220 --> 00:16:59,510
-Finally, I can
 read "Savage Love."

483
00:16:59,510 --> 00:17:00,010
-Argh!

484
00:17:00,010 --> 00:17:02,170
I spent a brief second
 without alcohol!

485
00:17:02,170 --> 00:17:04,890
[NEEDY GRUNTS]

486
00:17:04,890 --> 00:17:06,760
-Don't waste time
 with the cap, drink!

487
00:17:06,760 --> 00:17:07,260
OK.

488
00:17:07,260 --> 00:17:08,440
Now smoke, then drink.

489
00:17:08,440 --> 00:17:09,730
No, now answer the phone.

490
00:17:09,730 --> 00:17:10,440
Nngh!

491
00:17:10,440 --> 00:17:12,710
-Blackwood Agency.

492
00:17:12,710 --> 00:17:15,520
No, Mr. Alberts, I don't
 have any further information.

493
00:17:15,520 --> 00:17:17,100
-Hm.

494
00:17:17,100 --> 00:17:18,710
-Well, of course there's
 a search party out--

495
00:17:18,710 --> 00:17:19,420
[IMITATES WHIPCRACKING]
 ---there.

496
00:17:19,420 --> 00:17:22,470
I tell you, rescuers are
 looking all over for them.

497
00:17:22,470 --> 00:17:26,200
There's still hope,
 even after four days.

498
00:17:26,200 --> 00:17:27,090
Yes.

499
00:17:27,090 --> 00:17:30,040
I'll call you soon as
 I hear something new.

500
00:17:30,040 --> 00:17:30,770
-Hm.

501
00:17:30,770 --> 00:17:34,080
See, I was thinking of
 doing something-- ah, there.

502
00:17:39,350 --> 00:17:40,980
-(SINGING IN DEEP, GRAVELLY
 VOICE) Sun is rising.

503
00:17:51,790 --> 00:17:53,930
-Well, that didn't work
 out, so back to this shot.

504
00:17:57,460 --> 00:17:57,960
-Wow.

505
00:17:57,960 --> 00:17:59,610
Almost thought we were
 in trouble, there,

506
00:17:59,610 --> 00:18:02,170
flaming out and crashing at high
 speed into the ocean and all.

507
00:18:02,170 --> 00:18:03,450
[CRYING]

508
00:18:03,450 --> 00:18:04,890
-We've been in the
 water for an hour.

509
00:18:04,890 --> 00:18:06,300
Would you stop
 chewing on my foot?

510
00:18:12,300 --> 00:18:16,950
-(TEARFULLY) These survival
 meals are so high in fat.

511
00:18:16,950 --> 00:18:18,230
-Stop that bawling.

512
00:18:18,230 --> 00:18:20,600
You're driving us all nuts.

513
00:18:20,600 --> 00:18:22,770
-Debbie goes immediately
 into vacation mode.

514
00:18:25,400 --> 00:18:29,490
-Oh, when did they
 pick up kd lang?

515
00:18:29,490 --> 00:18:31,300
-Water.

516
00:18:31,300 --> 00:18:32,690
Just one swallow.

517
00:18:32,690 --> 00:18:34,370
-No more today.

518
00:18:34,370 --> 00:18:35,920
Tomorrow you'll
 get another ration.

519
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:36,660
-Of me.

520
00:18:39,470 --> 00:18:42,040
-Gary, give me some water.

521
00:18:44,810 --> 00:18:46,050
-Who do you think you are?

522
00:18:49,180 --> 00:18:52,410
-(FALSETTO) I'm trying
 to tan my shins.

523
00:18:52,410 --> 00:18:53,690
-The fifth night.

524
00:18:53,690 --> 00:18:56,300
We can't hold out much longer.

525
00:18:56,300 --> 00:18:57,500
-You're starting
 that, too, Georgia?

526
00:18:57,500 --> 00:18:59,570
-That darkens my features.

527
00:18:59,570 --> 00:19:01,630
-A ship has to go
 by here sometime.

528
00:19:01,630 --> 00:19:03,700
Or we've got to at
 least see some land.

529
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:07,340
-How big can the
 ocean be, anyway?

530
00:19:07,340 --> 00:19:12,550
-Oh, if only they could
 find a desalinization plant.

531
00:19:12,550 --> 00:19:13,940
-Water.

532
00:19:13,940 --> 00:19:15,340
Please give me water.

533
00:19:15,340 --> 00:19:19,050
-(FALSETTO) I need to
 rinse out a few things.

534
00:19:19,050 --> 00:19:20,480
-Look, a bird!

