00:56:50,330 --> 00:56:51,320
Mr. McFeely?

1594
00:56:51,320 --> 00:56:53,280
[GASPS]

1595
00:56:53,280 --> 00:56:56,220
-(FALSETTO) Oh.

1596
00:56:56,220 --> 00:56:57,580
-Gladys!

1597
00:56:57,580 --> 00:56:58,180
-Oh for goodness--

1598
00:56:58,180 --> 00:56:59,650
-Oh, Gladys.

1599
00:56:59,650 --> 00:57:03,480
-He attacked me, and
 now, we're engaged.

1600
00:57:03,480 --> 00:57:04,600
-Who's that?

1601
00:57:04,600 --> 00:57:06,990
-My friend Bob.

1602
00:57:06,990 --> 00:57:09,230
We work together
 for Professor Green.

1603
00:57:09,230 --> 00:57:11,400
-Why, he's a big
 uranium research man.

1604
00:57:11,400 --> 00:57:13,800
I met him before, in the reeds.

1605
00:57:13,800 --> 00:57:16,250
-A researching researcher?

1606
00:57:16,250 --> 00:57:16,750
-Hm?

1607
00:57:16,750 --> 00:57:20,160
-Then you had better luck
 than I. I was nearly shot.

1608
00:57:20,160 --> 00:57:20,810
-Hi, there.

1609
00:57:20,810 --> 00:57:22,830
-That is, if the
 revolver had been loaded.

1610
00:57:22,830 --> 00:57:23,610
-Tshaw.

1611
00:57:23,610 --> 00:57:24,390
Broads, huh?

1612
00:57:24,390 --> 00:57:25,870
-And you?

1613
00:57:25,870 --> 00:57:27,520
You here on a summer vacation?

1614
00:57:27,520 --> 00:57:28,030
-Hmph.

1615
00:57:28,030 --> 00:57:29,140
Fine summer vacation.

1616
00:57:29,140 --> 00:57:29,640
-Mm.

1617
00:57:29,640 --> 00:57:30,920
-If we hadn't found
 this island, we

1618
00:57:30,920 --> 00:57:32,490
wouldn't be here
 to tell the story.

1619
00:57:32,490 --> 00:57:35,560
We crashed on our
 way to Singapore.

1620
00:57:35,560 --> 00:57:38,100
-Ah, then you're the American
 dance troupe that got lost.

1621
00:57:38,100 --> 00:57:39,220
-Yes.

1622
00:57:39,220 --> 00:57:40,150
-Well, how do you know that?

1623
00:57:40,150 --> 00:57:42,180
-Oh, all the newspapers in the
 world have carried your story.

1624
00:57:42,180 --> 00:57:43,190
-What?
 -(FALSETTO) Huh?

1625
00:57:43,190 --> 00:57:44,490
-I'll bet Professor
 Green was surprised

1626
00:57:44,490 --> 00:57:46,400
when all these girls showed up.

1627
00:57:46,400 --> 00:57:47,290
Ha ha!

1628
00:57:47,290 --> 00:57:48,840
-Professor Green is dead.

1629
00:57:48,840 --> 00:57:49,520
-Well said.

1630
00:57:49,520 --> 00:57:50,600
-What's with the professor?

1631
00:57:50,600 --> 00:57:51,100
-Dead?

1632
00:57:51,100 --> 00:57:51,820
-What?

1633
00:57:51,820 --> 00:57:52,320
-Ha ha!

1634
00:57:52,320 --> 00:57:53,650
-He was hanging in
 a huge spider web.

1635
00:57:53,650 --> 00:57:54,270
That's how--
 -Jelly?

1636
00:57:54,270 --> 00:57:54,770
What?

1637
00:57:54,770 --> 00:57:55,620
---we found him.

1638
00:57:55,620 --> 00:57:58,380
Linda is dead, and Gary, our
 manager, has also disappeared.

1639
00:57:58,380 --> 00:57:58,960
-What's that?

1640
00:57:58,960 --> 00:57:59,460
Dead?

1641
00:57:59,460 --> 00:57:59,970
-Spider web?

1642
00:57:59,970 --> 00:58:00,470
-Dead?

1643
00:58:00,470 --> 00:58:01,940
-Do you really believe that?

1644
00:58:01,940 --> 00:58:04,730
By the way, our ship is
 coming back in two days.

1645
00:58:04,730 --> 00:58:06,470
-We must radio
 information right away so

1646
00:58:06,470 --> 00:58:08,410
that they can send an agent
 to clear the matter up.

1647
00:58:08,410 --> 00:58:08,910
-Dead?

1648
00:58:08,910 --> 00:58:09,470
-You mean it?

1649
00:58:09,470 --> 00:58:10,660
A ship's coming by?

1650
00:58:10,660 --> 00:58:11,900
-Our expedition ship.

1651
00:58:11,900 --> 00:58:12,950
-It will take us with it?

1652
00:58:12,950 --> 00:58:13,790
-Yeah, of course.

1653
00:58:13,790 --> 00:58:14,670
-We're rescued.

1654
00:58:14,670 --> 00:58:15,840
-Yeah.

1655
00:58:15,840 --> 00:58:16,830
-Not so fast, Gladys.

1656
00:58:16,830 --> 00:58:18,080
I have to sample the others.

1657
00:58:23,510 --> 00:58:24,140
-Wow.

1658
00:58:24,140 --> 00:58:24,990
She should depilate.

1659
00:58:24,990 --> 00:58:25,490
-Look.

1660
00:58:25,490 --> 00:58:26,560
Isn't it pretty?

1661
00:58:26,560 --> 00:58:28,060
-The boys will be
 surprised when they

1662
00:58:28,060 --> 00:58:30,380
see how we've dressed ourselves
 up in our island costumes.

1663
00:58:30,380 --> 00:58:30,880
-Boys?

1664
00:58:30,880 --> 00:58:33,450
Who's there, a Cub Scout troop?

1665
00:58:33,450 --> 00:58:35,410
-And they promised us
 real whiskey, kids.

1666
00:58:35,410 --> 00:58:37,030
I can hardly wait.

1667
00:58:37,030 --> 00:58:37,720
-Hold this.
 [WHIMPER]

1668
00:58:37,720 --> 00:58:38,930
-Shall I put this in my hair?
 -Uh-oh.

1669
00:58:38,930 --> 00:58:39,430
-Hey.

1670
00:58:39,430 --> 00:58:41,280
-You look great, just
 like a real islander.

1671
00:58:41,280 --> 00:58:42,210
-Well, I am.

1672
00:58:42,210 --> 00:58:43,380
Haven't I been here
 for four weeks?

1673
00:58:43,380 --> 00:58:43,900
[WHIMPERING]

1674
00:58:43,900 --> 00:58:45,480
-I can't believe
 that we'll be back

1675
00:58:45,480 --> 00:58:46,960
in New York in a couple of days.

1676
00:58:46,960 --> 00:58:49,300
-When we tell them at home
 what has happened to us,

1677
00:58:49,300 --> 00:58:50,830
nobody's going to believe us.

1678
00:58:50,830 --> 00:58:51,830
-Where are Georgia and Ann?

1679
00:58:51,830 --> 00:58:52,330
-Uh-oh.

1680
00:58:52,330 --> 00:58:53,390
-Outside on the veranda.

1681
00:58:53,390 --> 00:58:54,190
[SIGH]

1682
00:58:54,190 --> 00:58:55,300
-Mike, Servo's down again.

1683
00:58:55,300 --> 00:58:55,800
-Oh.

1684
00:58:55,800 --> 00:58:57,620
Uh, hey, Tom.

1685
00:58:57,620 --> 00:58:58,120
Wake up.

1686
00:58:58,120 --> 00:58:58,990
-I wonder if a lot--

1687
00:58:58,990 --> 00:58:59,570
-Come on, man.

1688
00:58:59,570 --> 00:59:00,180
-Sorry, Mike.

1689
00:59:00,180 --> 00:59:01,100
---when we get there.

1690
00:59:01,100 --> 00:59:01,800
-(OVERWHELMED) Flesh.

1691
00:59:01,800 --> 00:59:02,680
-Yeah.

1692
00:59:02,680 --> 00:59:03,630
[WHIMPERS]

1693
00:59:03,630 --> 00:59:05,870
-What's important for me
 is that Jimmy's there.

1694
00:59:05,870 --> 00:59:07,010
-Who is Jimmy?

1695
00:59:07,010 --> 00:59:07,560
-My friend.

1696
00:59:07,560 --> 00:59:08,700
We're going to be married.

1697
00:59:08,700 --> 00:59:09,870
-Even though he's invisible.

1698
00:59:09,870 --> 00:59:11,300
-What if he's got
 eyes for another?

1699
00:59:11,300 --> 00:59:13,170
-You don't believe
 that yourself.

1700
00:59:13,170 --> 00:59:17,050
I'd scratch his eyes
 out, and he knows it.

1701
00:59:17,050 --> 00:59:18,960
-Kids, we've made radio
 contact with the ship,

1702
00:59:18,960 --> 00:59:20,400
and we'll be picked up tomorrow!

1703
00:59:20,400 --> 00:59:23,570
-And tonight we'll dance
 and really raise the roof.

1704
00:59:23,570 --> 00:59:28,850
-Wow, these Amish barn raisings
 get wilder all the time.

1705
00:59:28,850 --> 00:59:30,170
-I'm going to call
 the rescue boat,

1706
00:59:30,170 --> 00:59:31,380
tell them to take their time.

1707
00:59:35,210 --> 00:59:35,710
-Huh.

1708
00:59:41,020 --> 00:59:42,740
-Next dance is with me, Ron.

1709
00:59:42,740 --> 00:59:43,240
Ha.

1710
00:59:47,030 --> 00:59:50,610
-Eh, she dances as well
 as Bruce Springsteen.

1711
00:59:50,610 --> 00:59:51,650
-(SINGING) This gun's for hire.

1712
00:59:55,070 --> 00:59:56,050
-(STRAINED) OK now.

1713
00:59:56,050 --> 00:59:56,970
Suck in the gut.

1714
00:59:56,970 --> 01:00:01,620
[INHALE]

1715
01:00:01,620 --> 01:00:04,790
-Unfortunately, she took lessons
 at the Arthur Miller dance

1716
01:00:04,790 --> 01:00:05,290
studio.

1717
01:00:05,290 --> 01:00:10,910
[SIGH]

1718
01:00:10,910 --> 01:00:13,880
-Well, I think I broke a
 rib tightening my belt.

1719
01:00:13,880 --> 01:00:16,300
-Tell me, what kind
 of a girl is Ann?

1720
01:00:16,300 --> 01:00:18,920
-Usually quiet and
 one of the nicest.

1721
01:00:18,920 --> 01:00:21,520
She's away from home
 for the first time.

1722
01:00:21,520 --> 01:00:23,610
Her folks live in Minnesota.

1723
01:00:23,610 --> 01:00:25,270
-Their governor's
 a huge, bald Nazi.

1724
01:00:28,090 --> 01:00:29,590
-I've never seen her so relaxed.

1725
01:00:29,590 --> 01:00:32,510
-Or puffy.

1726
01:00:32,510 --> 01:00:34,040
-I'd like to dance with her.

1727
01:00:34,040 --> 01:00:35,860
-But my crotch
 stuffing restricts me.

1728
01:00:39,350 --> 01:00:40,040
-Hi.

1729
01:00:40,040 --> 01:00:44,350
Been looking for a
 bloated man with a scarf?

1730
01:00:44,350 --> 01:00:45,810
-So, Minnesota.

1731
01:00:45,810 --> 01:00:46,840
That must be passive-agressive.

1732
01:00:52,000 --> 01:00:54,620
You know, anthropologists
 would call this a mating dance.

1733
01:00:54,620 --> 01:00:55,950
What do you say?

1734
01:00:55,950 --> 01:00:57,660
Heh heh.

1735
01:00:57,660 --> 01:00:59,530
-So, you into scouting?

1736
01:01:05,200 --> 01:01:05,760
-Twirling?

1737
01:01:05,760 --> 01:01:06,670
You must be mad!

1738
01:01:11,020 --> 01:01:13,810
-Mike, could you inject
 me into this film?

1739
01:01:13,810 --> 01:01:18,030
-I'm worried that Eric von
 Zipper's gonna show up.

1740
01:01:18,030 --> 01:01:19,480
-This is how I view the world.

1741
01:01:19,480 --> 01:01:22,210
Everyone else is out doing this
 while I sit in my underwear

1742
01:01:22,210 --> 01:01:26,080
eating a toasted cheese sandwich
 and drinking a nice beer.

1743
01:01:26,080 --> 01:01:27,050
-Pretty accurate.

1744
01:01:27,050 --> 01:01:28,070
-Come on, Bobby.

1745
01:01:28,070 --> 01:01:29,460
I want to be all alone with you.

1746
01:01:29,460 --> 01:01:30,350
-How 'bout the house?

1747
01:01:30,350 --> 01:01:31,050
-Not in the house.

1748
01:01:31,050 --> 01:01:32,470
Someplace where
 we'll be all alone.

1749
01:01:32,470 --> 01:01:32,970
-Wow.

1750
01:01:32,970 --> 01:01:34,410
-You know what?

1751
01:01:34,410 --> 01:01:35,740
We'll meet at the lagoon.

1752
01:01:35,740 --> 01:01:36,620
-OK.

1753
01:01:36,620 --> 01:01:37,980
But so that no one will notice.

1754
01:01:37,980 --> 01:01:39,100
-Good.

1755
01:01:39,100 --> 01:01:41,700
We'll meet in 15 minutes on
 the trail down to the water.

1756
01:01:41,700 --> 01:01:42,590
All set?

1757
01:01:42,590 --> 01:01:44,150
-Of course.

1758
01:01:44,150 --> 01:01:45,030
-Mwah.

1759
01:01:45,030 --> 01:01:46,040
-Oh, but be on time.

1760
01:01:46,040 --> 01:01:47,520
I hate waiting.

1761
01:01:47,520 --> 01:01:49,140
-Why can't this be
 on every channel,

1762
01:01:49,140 --> 01:01:51,700
like the State of
 the Union address?

1763
01:01:51,700 --> 01:01:52,570
It'd be great.

1764
01:01:58,010 --> 01:01:58,950
-Thanks.

1765
01:01:58,950 --> 01:01:59,620
-Hmm.

1766
01:01:59,620 --> 01:02:02,120
Women or booze, women or booze?

1767
01:02:02,120 --> 01:02:03,780
Hey, women and booze.

1768
01:02:03,780 --> 01:02:06,050
[WOLF WHISTLE]

1769
01:02:06,050 --> 01:02:07,540
-Having a good time
 with the chicks?

1770
01:02:07,540 --> 01:02:10,260
-Nah, mine's from Minnesota.

1771
01:02:10,260 --> 01:02:12,420
-Shut your mouth.

1772
01:02:12,420 --> 01:02:13,310
-Falling for her?

1773
01:02:13,310 --> 01:02:14,800
-Hmph.

1774
01:02:14,800 --> 01:02:21,670
[GLUGGING SOUNDS]

1775
01:02:21,670 --> 01:02:22,940
-Minnesota, huh?

1776
01:02:22,940 --> 01:02:27,850
Boy, that Harold Stassen is
 a bright young face, huh?

1777
01:02:27,850 --> 01:02:28,700
-Oh.

1778
01:02:28,700 --> 01:02:29,200
-Heh.

1779
01:02:29,200 --> 01:02:30,110
I drooled in it.

1780
01:02:30,110 --> 01:02:31,960
I hope that's OK.

1781
01:02:31,960 --> 01:02:34,170
-Thank you.

1782
01:02:34,170 --> 01:02:34,670
-Whoa.

1783
01:02:34,670 --> 01:02:36,070
Didn't think you'd
 drink that much.

1784
01:02:36,070 --> 01:02:37,000
Ah, well, hm.

1785
01:02:40,740 --> 01:02:44,490
-Tell me, are you
 always so quiet?

1786
01:02:44,490 --> 01:02:45,200
-Y-you know--

1787
01:02:45,200 --> 01:02:46,600
[DUMBFOUNDED GIGGLE]

1788
01:02:46,600 --> 01:02:48,430
---when you've live on
 islands for a long time,

1789
01:02:48,430 --> 01:02:50,650
you forget how to say
 nice things to girls.

1790
01:02:50,650 --> 01:02:52,430
-Well, try it once.

1791
01:02:52,430 --> 01:02:54,390
-Uh, do you like bugs?

1792
01:02:54,390 --> 01:02:57,380
-Well, I'm really glad
 that your airplane crashed.

1793
01:02:57,380 --> 01:02:59,130
-Well, that's a
 nice thing to say.

1794
01:02:59,130 --> 01:02:59,760
-I mean--

1795
01:02:59,760 --> 01:03:01,360
-I'm glad your puppy died.

1796
01:03:01,360 --> 01:03:01,970
I-- oh.

1797
01:03:01,970 --> 01:03:03,800
-I mean, otherwise, I wouldn't
 have gotten to know you.

1798
01:03:03,800 --> 01:03:06,640
And, and I'm glad about that.

1799
01:03:06,640 --> 01:03:08,810
-Oh, you say that to everybody.

1800
01:03:08,810 --> 01:03:09,740
-No, Ann.

1801
01:03:09,740 --> 01:03:11,100
I don't know any girls.

1802
01:03:11,100 --> 01:03:11,600
-Eh-heh.

1803
01:03:11,600 --> 01:03:14,200
-And when I think of the
 ones I do, I'd rather work.

1804
01:03:14,200 --> 01:03:15,910
-Well, I must say,
 you certainly are

1805
01:03:15,910 --> 01:03:18,430
a master at paying compliments.

1806
01:03:18,430 --> 01:03:21,410
-Perhaps you're--
 perhaps you're right.

1807
01:03:21,410 --> 01:03:23,560
I should pay more
 attention to young women.

1808
01:03:23,560 --> 01:03:25,480
-Maybe I wouldn't be so gay.

1809
01:03:25,480 --> 01:03:27,460
-Sometimes I envy Bob.

1810
01:03:27,460 --> 01:03:30,220
He's so uninhibited
 and carefree.

1811
01:03:30,220 --> 01:03:31,230
-Bob?

1812
01:03:31,230 --> 01:03:32,820
Any girl is good enough for him.

1813
01:03:32,820 --> 01:03:34,200
[LEERING LAUGH]

1814
01:03:34,200 --> 01:03:35,590
[MUSIC PLAYING]

1815
01:03:40,450 --> 01:03:43,900
-A real man is interested
 in only one girl.

1816
01:03:43,900 --> 01:03:47,910
-Then I'm a-- then
 I'm a real man.

1817
01:03:47,910 --> 01:03:50,210
-How?

1818
01:03:50,210 --> 01:03:50,920
-Ow!

1819
01:03:50,920 --> 01:03:54,540
-Because I'm really
 interested only in one girl.

1820
01:03:54,540 --> 01:03:55,900
-Who's that?

1821
01:03:55,900 --> 01:03:57,160
What does she look like?

1822
01:03:57,160 --> 01:03:57,660
Cute?

1823
01:03:57,660 --> 01:03:58,830
Elegant?

1824
01:03:58,830 --> 01:03:59,490
-Elegant?

1825
01:03:59,490 --> 01:04:00,790
Mm, on the contrary.

1826
01:04:00,790 --> 01:04:01,640
-Hmm.

1827
01:04:01,640 --> 01:04:04,530
-A little bit disheveled
 and neglected,

1828
01:04:04,530 --> 01:04:07,580
but, um, very pretty.

1829
01:04:07,580 --> 01:04:08,570
-Oh no, I didn't mean you.

1830
01:04:08,570 --> 01:04:09,070
Sorry.

1831
01:04:09,070 --> 01:04:10,600
-Her name's Ann.

1832
01:04:10,600 --> 01:04:12,890
-Oh, Joe.

1833
01:04:12,890 --> 01:04:16,220
-That's one of the nicest things
 that's ever been said to me.

1834
01:04:16,220 --> 01:04:19,090
-Well, I meant it.

1835
01:04:19,090 --> 01:04:20,200
I'm really fond of you.

1836
01:04:20,200 --> 01:04:23,310
-In a one night
 stand sort of way.

1837
01:04:23,310 --> 01:04:28,360
-You know, whe-- when you
 leave for New York, here.

1838
01:04:28,360 --> 01:04:29,880
So you won't forget
 me too quickly.

1839
01:04:29,880 --> 01:04:32,590
-(FALSETTO) Get this sweaty
 thing off me, you creep.

1840
01:04:32,590 --> 01:04:37,640
-But Joe, please don't
 let me wait too long.

1841
01:04:37,640 --> 01:04:39,760
-(FALSETTO) Ow,
 that really hurt.

1842
01:04:39,760 --> 01:04:40,260
-Come on.

1843
01:04:40,260 --> 01:04:42,230
I got 12 minutes left.

1844
01:04:42,230 --> 01:04:43,770
-What's in 12 minutes?

1845
01:04:43,770 --> 01:04:44,270
-My next--

1846
01:04:44,270 --> 01:04:45,140
[IMITATES ACCENT]

1847
01:04:45,140 --> 01:04:47,340
[MOANING]

1848
01:04:47,340 --> 01:04:48,900
-Are you attracted to
 my Jeff Conaway hair?

1849
01:04:52,130 --> 01:04:53,620
BABS (OFFSCREEN):
 Hello, big boy.

1850
01:04:53,620 --> 01:04:56,030
-Oh, hello, Jessica Rabbit.

1851
01:04:56,030 --> 01:04:57,320
-Well, I'll be darned.

1852
01:04:57,320 --> 01:05:00,610
That's not bad, either.

1853
01:05:00,610 --> 01:05:01,680
Hey, all right.

1854
01:05:01,680 --> 01:05:04,990
You really don't know
 where to start here.

1855
01:05:04,990 --> 01:05:07,930
-(FALSETTO) I tried to tackle
